WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.920 --> 00:00:06.060
We humans like to think of ourselves as&nbsp;
the undisputed masters of planet earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.320
We have ardously evolved, over&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands of years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:17.880
the advanced adaptations required to survive&nbsp;
in a slightly warm living room. With the aid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:22.920
of advanced technology our civilisation&nbsp;
has settled in every petrified pocket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:30.000
and withered wasteland on this wicked world.
 
Except, that’s not entirely true - as of today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.020
humans can be found in significantly less than&nbsp;
1% of all the liveable locations on earth.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:42.300
At first that might sound hard to&nbsp;
believe... aside from the tallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:47.400
mountains, the hottest deserts and that&nbsp;
Brazilian island full of fucking snakes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:52.320
we've built permanent settlements&nbsp;
pretty much everywhere… haven’t we?

00:00:52.980 --> 00:01:00.720
Actually, not even close, for the simple reason&nbsp;
that well over 99% of the planet’s habitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:08.580
space is found in the world's oceans. And as&nbsp;
of today, we've colonised precisely none of it.

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.180
From our 'terra firma-first' perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:17.580
it's easy to underestimate the sheer scale&nbsp;
of the underwater world. There are over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.580 --> 00:01:22.620
billion cubic kilometres of water in earth's&nbsp;
combined oceans, and the Mariana Trench,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:28.380
the deepest point on the ocean floor, is a&nbsp;
staggering 6.8 miles beneath the surface.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.300
Just saying the number - 6.8 miles&nbsp;
- it doesn’t really give you a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.780
of just how far down we’re talking&nbsp;
here, so let me make a comparison.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.300
The world's tallest building, the Burj Khalifa&nbsp;
in Dubai, is almost twice the height of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:51.060
Empire State Building. And yet, you could stack&nbsp;
*13* Burj Khalifa's one on top of the other in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:56.460
the Mariana Trench and you *still* wouldn't&nbsp;
break the surface of the Pacific ocean above.

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:01.380
That, dear Thoughty2 viewer,&nbsp;
is absolutely bloody mental.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:29.400
Most of the ocean is significantly shallower&nbsp;
than the Mariana Trench, but the global average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:35.220
depth is still a healthy 2.3 miles, and&nbsp;
pretty much all of that space is habitable.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:39.960
Considering its sheer size it should come as&nbsp;
no surprise that the ocean is home to some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:46.200
78% of earth's total animal biomass. Near&nbsp;
the surface, that biomass comes in the form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:51.960
of animals that are fairly familiar - whales,&nbsp;
sharks, brightly coloured fish, sexy mermaids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:58.140
that sort of thing. But dive a little deeper,&nbsp;
and a deluge of disturbing creatures await.

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:08.340
At 400 metres you’ll meet the monstrous&nbsp;
Goblin Shark whose extendable jaws can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:11.220
catapulted forwards to engulf unsuspecting prey.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.060 --> 00:04:19.440
Descend to around 1000 metres and a humorous&nbsp;
respite awaits you in the form of the blobfish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:25.080
multiple winner of the ugliest animal alive&nbsp;
award (that’s actually a thing by the way).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:31.800
Sink further still into the shadowy depths,&nbsp;
to 2000 metres below and you’ll encounter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.280
the ghastly gulper eel, a creature that brings new&nbsp;
meaning to the phrase 'all mouth and no trousers.'

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:45.540
But it isn’t just weird fish you’ll find in&nbsp;
this watery abyss - nature’s wettest workshop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:51.360
also breeds giants. From the Kraken to&nbsp;
Jörmungand, we've been telling stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.760
of sea monsters for thousands of years. And&nbsp;
perhaps there’s good reason for that. Because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.520
thanks to a phenomenon known as deep sea&nbsp;
gigantism, many creatures that have evolved to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:08.820
live in the darkest ocean depths are significantly&nbsp;
larger than similar species found in the shallows.

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.300
Probably the most famous example of Deep&nbsp;
Sea Gigantism is the giant squid. Reaching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:22.020
a maximum length of 13 metres and weighing&nbsp;
in at over 250 kilograms, this substantial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:27.540
cephalopod is literally hundreds of times&nbsp;
larger than many of its shallow-water cousins.

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:30.600
The giant squid is a genuine monster of the deep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:35.760
but it turns out this great giant has a great&nbsp;
big brother - a much, much bigger brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:43.140
the aptly named colossal squid. Weighing as much&nbsp;
as *three times* more than the already enormous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:49.440
giant squid, the colossal squid is the largest&nbsp;
invertebrate on planet earth, with eyes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:55.560
size of basketballs and hooked suckers capable&nbsp;
of leaving sperm whales with permanent scars.

00:05:55.560 --> 00:06:01.620
But if that doesn’t freak you out, here’s a&nbsp;
subsurface specimen that’ll give you mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:08.520
scars. This “little” critter is a giant&nbsp;
isopod. If it looks strangely familiar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:13.260
that's probably because it's closely related&nbsp;
to the humble woodlouse. A well-fed woodlouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.480
is about a centimetre long and weighs less&nbsp;
than a gram soaking wet. The giant isopod,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:23.700
on the other hand, can grow close to half&nbsp;
a metre and weighs almost 2 kilograms.

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:27.120
Everywhere we look in the deepest,&nbsp;
darkest depths of the ocean,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:33.360
we see a similar story - familiar-looking&nbsp;
animals have been mysteriously dino-sized.

00:06:33.360 --> 00:06:40.620
Like the Japanese Spider Crab with a leg span of&nbsp;
up to 4 metres, and the giant oarfish, sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:47.220
known as the King of Herrings, which can reach&nbsp;
a maximum length of around 11 metres. 11 metres!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:52.740
It’s like God turned up to work drunk on the&nbsp;
fifth day and made a bunch of rounding errors.

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.600
So, just what the hell is going on here? What’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:00.720
causing deep sea species to&nbsp;
grow so freakishly large?

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.600
The truth is, we actually aren't 100% sure.&nbsp;
As you're probably already aware, we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:12.840
surprisingly little about the ocean floor. In&nbsp;
fact, up until 2019 more people had walked on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:18.360
surface of the moon than had visited the deepest&nbsp;
part of the Mariana Trench, Challenger Deep.

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.420
The sheer remoteness of deep ocean environments&nbsp;
makes them - and the creatures that live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.420 --> 00:07:31.020
there - incredibly difficult to study. Take the&nbsp;
colossal squid as a prime example. As long as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.020 --> 00:07:36.000
a London bus and weighing more than a fully&nbsp;
grown polar bear you'd think it’d be pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.000 --> 00:07:43.080
bloody hard to miss. And yet, with all our fancy&nbsp;
technology and remote operated submersibles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:50.040
not a single colossal squid has *ever* been&nbsp;
observed in its natural habitat. Not one!

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:56.400
Since we never get to bag ourselves one of these&nbsp;
beasties, research into the causes of deep-sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:01.560
gigantism has proved rather difficult -&nbsp;
but we do have some surprising theories.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:07.740
The first is that food scarcity is&nbsp;
the primary cause. The idea that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:13.560
*lack* of food could somehow stimulate the&nbsp;
evolution of larger animals might sound&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:17.820
like nonsense - surely the opposite&nbsp;
would happen, right? But dig a little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:22.380
deeper into the science and this apparent&nbsp;
contradiction starts to make a lot of sense.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:27.960
Unlike almost every other ecosystem&nbsp;
on the planet, the ocean depths have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:34.260
close to zero food production of their&nbsp;
own - no light means no photosynthesis,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.260 --> 00:08:41.820
and no photosynthesis means no phytoplankton,&nbsp;
which is the main producer near the surface. As&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.820 --> 00:08:49.020
a result, the primary food source in the deep sea&nbsp;
is so-called marine snow. A deep ocean delicacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.020 --> 00:08:55.500
marine snow is basically a mix of dead animals,&nbsp;
decaying phytoplankton, and a whole load of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.500 --> 00:09:01.500
fish shit that slowly drifts down into the abyss&nbsp;
from the shallow waters above. Sounds delicious!

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:08.520
Marine snow - supplemented by the carcasses&nbsp;
of larger animals - is consumed as it falls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.520 --> 00:09:14.340
meaning the deeper you get the less there is&nbsp;
to go around. That makes food on the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.340 --> 00:09:18.600
bottom both scarce and unpredictable&nbsp;
- there isn't a great deal of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.600 --> 00:09:21.420
and you never know where the&nbsp;
juiciest morsels will land.

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:27.120
For a bottom-dwelling scavenger like&nbsp;
the giant isopod, being bigger means&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:31.200
it can cover longer distances more&nbsp;
quickly, increasing its foraging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:38.400
radius and improving its chances of being&nbsp;
the first on the scene when food arrives.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.920
Larger animals are also able to&nbsp;
store more food than smaller ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:47.880
helping them to survive long&nbsp;
periods without sustenance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.440 --> 00:09:54.240
Many deep sea creatures have evolved extendable&nbsp;
stomachs that allow them to take in the maximum&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.240 --> 00:10:02.220
amount of food on the rare occasions a solid meal&nbsp;
presents itself. The results can be surprising - a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.220 --> 00:10:09.900
giant isopod with the catchy nickname 'Giant&nbsp;
Isopod #1' once went 5 years without eating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:16.440
a single thing, presumably in protest at its&nbsp;
imprisonment in the Toba aquarium in Japan.

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:23.160
5 years without food is undeniably&nbsp;
supermodel-tier dieting, but these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.160 --> 00:10:28.200
long fasts are made possible by another benefit&nbsp;
of increased size - a more efficient metabolism.

00:10:28.200 --> 00:10:34.620
According to Kleiber's law, an animal's metabolic&nbsp;
rate scales to the 3⁄4 power of its mass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:38.100
To translate that into normal person English,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:42.960
larger animals need less energy&nbsp;
per unit of mass than smaller ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.040 --> 00:10:49.200
A mouse for example needs to eat up to 20% of&nbsp;
its body weight every single day to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:57.540
whereas an elephant only needs to consume 4 or 5%&nbsp;
of its body weight. In other words, larger animals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.540 --> 00:11:04.620
use energy more efficiently. And that is a massive&nbsp;
bonus in an environment where food is scarce.

00:11:06.420 --> 00:11:10.440
The other main theory as to why creatures of&nbsp;
the deep grow so big is related to temperature.

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:16.740
Because cold water is denser and therefore&nbsp;
heavier than warm water it sinks, and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.740 --> 00:11:21.660
means deep-sea environments are extremely&nbsp;
cold. Frigid temperatures inhibit the speed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.660 --> 00:11:27.420
of metabolic processes, decreasing the&nbsp;
amount of food and oxygen required to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:31.620
sustain life. A slower metabolism is also&nbsp;
thought to be part of the reason many deep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.620 --> 00:11:36.960
sea creatures grow slower and live longer&nbsp;
than similar species in shallower waters.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:44.880
As an extreme example, recent research suggests&nbsp;
that the bottom-dwelling Greenland Shark may be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:52.620
able to live to well over 500 years old, making&nbsp;
it the longest-living vertebrate ever discovered.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:59.340
Just think about that for a second - there may&nbsp;
be a Greenland Shark swimming around today that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.340 --> 00:12:05.220
was alive when Leonardo Da Vinci painted&nbsp;
the Mona Lisa in the early 16th century.

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:12.060
The idea that lower temperatures play a role&nbsp;
in deep-sea gigantism is backed up by the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.060 --> 00:12:18.000
we *also* see something called *polar* gigantism&nbsp;
- a tendency for species that live in the frigid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.000 --> 00:12:24.600
waters of the Arctic and Southern oceans to&nbsp;
exhibit gigantism, even in shallower waters.

00:12:24.600 --> 00:12:30.000
Sea spiders are a good example - in most parts&nbsp;
of the world, sea spiders are relatively tiny,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:37.080
from a few millimetres to a couple of centimetres&nbsp;
across. The Antarctic sea spider, on the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:43.680
hand, has a nightmare-inducing 70-centimetre leg&nbsp;
span - easily big enough to latch onto your face&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.680 --> 00:12:48.540
and inject thousands of its babies directly down&nbsp;
your throat. Similar massive increases in body&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.540 --> 00:12:54.360
size can be seen in polar sponges, worms,&nbsp;
starfish, and even single-celled organisms.

00:12:55.760 --> 00:13:02.040
OK, so we've got the food scarcity theory and the&nbsp;
temperature theory, but there's also a far simpler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:07.560
reason deep-sea species have evolved to get so&nbsp;
big - there are significantly fewer predators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.560 --> 00:13:14.280
down there. That's allowed Mother Nature to be&nbsp;
a little bolder with the body sizes of species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:20.820
that might traditionally stay small to attract&nbsp;
less attention from things that want to eat them.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:26.460
The truth is, there's probably no single&nbsp;
unifying explanation for deep sea gigantism,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.900
and different variables will play a bigger role&nbsp;
in some species than others. It's also worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.900 --> 00:13:36.060
pointing out that plenty of abyssal creatures&nbsp;
exhibit the exact opposite effect - deep sea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:42.480
dwarfism - which is a clear indicator that this&nbsp;
little puzzle is unlikely to have a simple answer.

00:13:42.480 --> 00:13:48.300
Ultimately, the deep ocean is a chance&nbsp;
to see how life somehow finds a way in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.840
one of the most inhospitable&nbsp;
environments on planet earth.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:55.920
Thanks for watching.

